{"id":1058,"date":"2026-03-23T08:18:24","date_gmt":"2026-03-23T08:18:24","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:10005\/?p=1058"},"modified":"2026-03-23T09:23:34","modified_gmt":"2026-03-23T09:23:34","slug":"%e6%94%af%e4%bb%98%e9%80%9a%e9%81%93%e7%bd%91%e7%bb%9c%e4%b8%ad%e5%ae%89%e5%85%a8%e4%b8%94%e5%8c%bf%e5%90%8d%e7%9a%84%e5%a4%9a%e9%80%9a%e9%81%93%e6%9b%b4%e6%96%b0%e5%8d%8f%e8%ae%ae","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/en\/%e6%94%af%e4%bb%98%e9%80%9a%e9%81%93%e7%bd%91%e7%bb%9c%e4%b8%ad%e5%ae%89%e5%85%a8%e4%b8%94%e5%8c%bf%e5%90%8d%e7%9a%84%e5%a4%9a%e9%80%9a%e9%81%93%e6%9b%b4%e6%96%b0%e5%8d%8f%e8%ae%ae\/","title":{"rendered":"Secure and Anonymous Multi-Channel Update Protocol for Payment Channel Networks"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3749991f wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"min-height:1000px\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-458a08b5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\"><h2 class=\"lab-paper-title wp-block-post-title\">Secure and Anonymous Multi-Channel Update Protocol for Payment Channel Networks<\/h2>\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">WANG J, GAO S,&nbsp;<strong>LI G<\/strong>,<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><em>IEEE Transactions on Information Forensics and Security<\/em><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons lab-btn-group is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/SAMCU_Secure_and_Anonymous_Multi-Channel_Updates_in_Payment_Channel_Networks.pdf\">\ud83d\udcc4 PDF<\/a><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-fill\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https:\/\/ieeexplore.ieee.org\/abstract\/document\/10654379\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/LWC.2023.3330809 noreferrer noopener\">\ud83d\udd17 DOI Link<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-text-color has-contrast-3-color has-alpha-channel-opacity has-contrast-3-background-color has-background is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46005010 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udccc Abstract<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote has-text-align-left lab-abstract-quote has-small-font-size\" style=\"margin-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);margin-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0\"><blockquote><p>Payment channel networks (PCNs) have become the mainstream solution to Bitcoin's scalability problem, significantly reducing on-chain transaction burden through efficient off-chain updates. However, existing update protocols exhibit apparent conflicts when simultaneously pursuing security, privacy, and expressiveness. This paper proposes a novel UTXO-based off-chain update protocol\u2014the Secure and Anonymous Multi-Channel Update protocol. SAMCU is the first to simultaneously achieve internal anonymity (sender\/receiver privacy), balance security, and multi-channel updates. To achieve internal anonymity, this paper introduces update graph segmentation, which partitions the update graph into chain-like subgraphs so that each participant only knows the identities of parties within its own subgraph, rather than the addresses of all participants in the entire update graph. To address the balance security issue caused by equivalent subgraphs in the naive UGS scheme, this paper further proposes the Enabled Payment Transaction Tree, which guarantees the balance security of every honest participant through carefully designed time locks and signature locks. Furthermore, SAMCU is also optimized in performance by reducing transaction fees through split transactions and reducing the number of communication connections through a hierarchical communication strategy. Prototype experiments (involving up to 100 update payment channels) show that when the number of update channels is 100, SAMCU saves approximately 70% of communication connections and reduces on-chain transaction fees by approximately 66% compared to the existing optimal scheme.<\/p><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6adec98e lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udca1 Core Contributions<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<ul start=\"1\" style=\"border-style:none;border-width:0px\" class=\"wp-block-list lab-feature-list\">\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>First to propose a UTXO protocol that simultaneously achieves MCU, internal anonymity, and constant collateral<\/strong>: Existing UTXO schemes (e.g., AMCU, Thora) require all participants to share each other's public addresses to collaboratively generate enabling payment transactions, allowing intermediate parties to easily infer the identities of the sender and receiver; HTLC schemes offer anonymity but only support multi-hop single-path payments (MHP) and face the linear collateral problem. SAMCU partitions the update graph into subgraphs via UGS, enabling each party to know only the identity information within its own subgraph, thereby achieving internal anonymity without sacrificing MCU capability or constant collateral, filling a gap in this field (as shown in Table I of the original paper).<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Proposes EPTT to address the balance security issue in the naive UGS scheme<\/strong>: In the naive UGS scheme, a malicious trigger can delay the publication of txep1 until after <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>\u2212<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u0394<\/mi><mo>\u2212<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">T \u2013 3\u0394 \u2013 t_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, causing receivers in other subgraphs to be unable to complete on-chain forced payment due to insufficient time, thereby breaking atomicity. EPTT constructs a transaction tree for each channel receiver with that receiver as the trigger, constraining the behavior of rational triggers through hierarchical time lock settings (txep1 must be published before <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>\u2212<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u0394<\/mi><mo>\u2212<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">T \u2013 5\u0394 \u2013 t_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> ); meanwhile, double spending is prevented through carefully designed multi-signature conditions, and the Child-Pays-For-Parent (CPFP) mechanism is employed to distribute on-chain transaction fees across each party's own payment transactions, rather than forcing each party to bear the cost of the entire tree.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Proves the balance security and internal anonymity of SAMCU<\/strong>: Theorem 1 enumerates all possible state combinations (unchanged\/revoked\/compensated\/successful) of the upstream channel <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>\u03b3<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\u03b3_l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> and downstream channel <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>\u03b3<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\u03b3_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> , deriving contradictions one by one to prove that honest intermediate parties do not lose funds; Theorem 2 proves that under the undisputed honest execution path, the temporary addresses of key parties (KPs) do not appear on-chain, so each party only knows the public addresses within its own subgraph, and the protocol achieves internal anonymity.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-2f9d57f4 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udd27 Method Overview<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-b46d2053 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-22-30-1024x443.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-22-30.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-22-30.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_17-22-30-1024x443.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1089\" width=\"1024\" height=\"443\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_17-22-30\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-4ef57bdf wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-12-37-278x300.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-12-37.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-12-37.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-12-37-278x300.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1060\" width=\"400\" height=\"400\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_16-12-37\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-container-content-9dbf05af wp-block-paragraph\">The PCN is modeled as a weighted graph <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo separator=\"true\">,<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">G := (V, E)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, where the node set <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> represents user blockchain addresses and the edge set <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> represents opened payment channels.<strong>The SAMCU protocol<\/strong>is executed in six phases:<strong>Initialization<\/strong>phase: the Dealer performs update graph segmentation and generates parameters required for txrelay;<strong>Pre-Setup-SG<\/strong> phase: each receiver <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo separator=\"true\">,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">R_{i,j}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>generates txin and txep1, where txep1 contains only the outputs of receivers within the same subgraph and one special output for cross-subgraph notification;<strong>Pre-Setup-UG<\/strong> phase: the key party generates txrelay and txep2 and distributes the relevant parameters to each subgraph;<strong>Setup<\/strong> phase: the channel sender generates txstate, txrefund, and txp1\/p2;<strong>Confirmation<\/strong> phase: a two-step notification mechanism ensures that all channels have completed state updates and all receivers hold valid txep1 before entering the final phase;<strong>Finalization<\/strong> phase: off-chain state updates are completed, and if a dispute arises, the protocol enters the <strong>Response<\/strong> phase, where each receiver enforces payment on-chain according to the EPTT tree structure. The UGS partitioning principle requires that senders and receivers must serve as key parties, each subgraph must contain at least one segment of each chain, and the update graph must be partitioned into at least three subgraphs to prevent intermediate parties from simultaneously learning the identities of the sender and receiver.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-dda34f26 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcca Experimental Results<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-080de2b0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-16-06.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-16-06.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-16-06.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-16-06.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1062\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_16-16-06\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><figcaption class=\"uagb-image-caption\">Fig.9<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-080de2b0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-15-58.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-15-58.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-15-58.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-15-58.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1061\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_16-15-58\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><figcaption class=\"uagb-image-caption\">Fig.8<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>When the number of update channels is 100 and the number of subgraphs is 10, SAMCU reduces the number of communication connections from 5350 to 1570 compared to Thora (a reduction of approximately 70%), and reduces on-chain transaction fees from 9.2 USD to 3.1 USD (a reduction of approximately 66%), with the key party's computation time not exceeding 1.5 seconds.<\/strong>: As shown in <strong>Fig. 8<\/strong> As shown, as the number of payment channels increases from 20 to 100, the total on-chain transaction size of each scheme grows approximately linearly, while under the same number of channels, SAMCU's transaction size decreases significantly as the number of subgraphs increases;<strong>The convergence curves in Fig. 9<\/strong> The computation time results show that the key party's computation time grows quadratically but remains within 1.5 seconds in the 100-channel scenario;<strong>Fig. 10<\/strong> The communication connection comparison verifies the significant effectiveness of the hierarchical communication strategy, reducing communication connections by approximately 70% under the 100-channel, 10-subgraph condition.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-c1c6bca8 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-16-16.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-16-16.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-16-16.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-16-16.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1063\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_16-16-16\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><figcaption class=\"uagb-image-caption\">Fig.10<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-e3c2d50d lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcdd Citation<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code lab-bibtex-code has-small-font-size\"><code>@article{wang2024samcu,\n  author    = {Jianhuan Wang and Shang Gao and Guyue Li and Keke Gai and Bin Xiao},\n  title     = {{SAMCU}: Secure and Anonymous Multi-Channel Updates in Payment Channel Networks},\n  journal   = {IEEE Transactions on Information Forensics and Security},\n  volume    = {19},\n  pages     = {9115--9128},\n  year      = {2024},\n  doi       = {10.1109\/TIFS.2024.3451366}\n}\n<\/code><\/pre>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div style=\"height:100px\" aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-spacer\"><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-content-justification-space-between is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-788841da wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Payment channel networks (PCNs) have become a mainstream solution to Bitcoin's scalability issues, significantly reducing on-chain transaction burdens through efficient off-chain updates. However, existing update protocols exhibit notable conflicts when simultaneously pursuing security, privacy, and expressiveness. This paper proposes a novel UTXO-based off-chain update protocol\u2014the Secure and Anonymous Multi-Channel Update protocol (SAMCU). SAMCU achieves, for the first time in history, the simultaneous realization of three goals: internal anonymity (sender\/receiver privacy), balance security, and multi-channel updates (MCU). To achieve internal anonymity, this paper introduces the Update Graph Segmentation (UGS) technique, which partitions the update graph into several chain-like subgraphs (SGs), ensuring that each participant only knows the identities of parties within their own subgraph, rather than the addresses of all participants in the entire update graph. To address the balance security issues caused by equivalent subgraphs in the naive UGS scheme, this paper further proposes the Enabled Payment Transaction Tree (EPTT), which safeguards the balance security of every honest participant through carefully designed time locks and signature lock conditions. Additionally, by splitting transactions to reduce transaction fees and employing a hierarchical communication strategy to reduce the number of communication connections, SAMCU is also optimized in performance. Prototype system experiments (involving up to 100 updated payment channels) demonstrate that when the number of updated channels is 100, SAMCU can save approximately 70% of communication connections and reduce on-chain transaction fees by about 66% compared to the existing optimal scheme.<\/p>","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":1060,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"mypost","format":"standard","meta":{"_uag_custom_page_level_css":"","footnotes":""},"categories":[5],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-1058","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-research"],"uagb_featured_image_src":{"full":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-12-37.png",636,686,false],"thumbnail":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-12-37-150x150.png",150,150,true],"medium":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_16-12-37-278x300.png",278,300,true],"medium_large":["https:\/\/jiushi